   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "## Summary\n\nI completed this search with minimal exploration, and here's my reasoning:\n\n1. **Declared triggers upfront**: `best_so_far_above(1.2)` \u2192 stop, `failures_at_least(2)` \u2192 stop, `last_gain_at_most(0.02)` \u2192 stop.\n\n2. **First move (`init`)**: Anchored on the best single feature out of 80 candidates (40 features \u00d7 2 signs), which immediately yielded an in-sample Sharpe of **1.2403** \u2014 already above my 1.2 stopping bar.\n\n3. **Stopped immediately**: Rather than spending my remaining content moves (`extend_best`, `swap_worst`, etc.) to chase a higher in-sample number, I honored the trigger that fired on the very first move. This is a deliberate choice: a specification selected with *zero* search beyond picking the single best feature carries far less multiple-testing/overfitting risk than one assembled after several rounds of combination-searching, even though such a combination might post a higher in-sample Sharpe.\n\n4. **Final specification**: `[33-]` \u2014 a single feature, held short, as an equal-weight (trivially, 100%-weight) combination.\n\n5. **Prediction**: mean OOS Sharpe 0.60, sd 0.45 \u2014 reflecting substantial expected shrinkage from the in-sample 1.24 (a common and conservative discount for single-feature signals), balanced against the fact that no additional search was performed to inflate the in-sample number artificially.\n\nSubmitted exactly once, as required."
